#46 — tokens issued or revoked on one node take effect on every node sharing the store
2026-08-23 · rig deploy/compose/timelakedb-cluster-s3.yml, brought up with
TLDB_DATA_AUTH=required (router :5970, ingester-a :5971, ingester-b :5972,
querier-a :5973, one LocalStack bucket) · drill cluster-drill/token_reload_drill.sh
branch token-reload on top of cd18a36 (#45)
================================================================================

THE CLAIM: a token issued on ingester-a's console authenticates on ingester-b
and on querier-a without either restarting — on first presentation (the
on-miss re-read) and in any case within one maintenance tick — and a token
REVOKED on ingester-a stops working on both within one tick. Before this
change every node read catalog/config/tokens.json exactly once, at open, so
the file was shared and the in-memory copy was not: a token issued on A was
401 on B until B restarted, and a token revoked on A kept working on B, which
is not revocation.

UNIT: crates/auth/src/lib.rs
  tokens_issued_and_revoked_on_one_node_reach_another_through_the_store
  an_unknown_token_triggers_one_bounded_reload_so_a_fresh_token_works_on_first_use
  — the first one went RED on the first gate run for a real reason: on a
    fresh store `open` recorded no hash for the absent file, so the first
    reload counted "nothing → empty file" as a change. An absent file now
    hashes as the empty file. 23/23 after.

Mechanism: one `get` of tokens.json per maintenance tick per node (the
querier on its catalog tail, the only periodic thing it has), hashed before
it is parsed; plus one re-read on an unknown token, rate-limited to one per
second node-wide so a flood of bad tokens costs the store one read a second.
timelake_auth_token_reloads_total counts the reloads that changed the set.

